[38;2;124;124;124mfunctions.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThis is a classic problem in measure theory / real analysis. The pointwise limit[0m[38;2;124;124;124mof a sequence of measurable functions is measurable. So any non-measurable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction would work.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe key theorem: If $f_n$ are measurable functions and $f_n \to f$ pointwise, [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mthen $f$ is measurable. This is because the pointwise limit of measurable [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunctions is measurable.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mSo we need a non-measurable function. The classic example is the indicator [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mfunction of a non-measurable set (like a Vitali set).[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mLet me write the proof.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mFirst, the answer leak check: The problem statement just asks to identify such a[0m[38;2;124;124;124mfunction. No answer is leaked.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124mThe proof:[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m1. Recall that the pointwise limit of a sequence of measurable functions is [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeasurable. This is a standard theorem.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m2. Therefore, any non-measurable function $f: \mathbb{R} \to \mathbb{R}$ cannot [0m[38;2;124;124;124mbe expressed as the pointwise limit of a sequence of measurable functions.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m3. To construct such a function, we use a Vitali set $V \subset \mathbb{R}$. A [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mVitali set is a set of representatives of the equivalence classes of $[0m
[38;2;124;124;124m\mathbb{R}$ under the relation $x \sim y \iff x - y \in \mathbb{Q}$. By Vitali's[0m[38;2;124;124;124mtheorem, $V$ is not Lebesgue measurable.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m4. Define $f = \mathbf{1}_V$, the indicator function of $V$.[0m

[38;2;124;124;124m5. If $f$ were the pointwise limit of measurable functions, then $f$ would be [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mmeasurable, which would mean $V = f^{-1}(\{1\})$ is a measurable set — [0m
[38;2;124;124;124mcontradiction.[0m

